          "text": "Issues to check:\n1. In `Invoke-ShowLogin`, `$values['email']` — `$values` is a hashtable with keys email and next. `[string]$Ctx.FormData['email']` — if FormData is empty hashtable, `[string]@{}['email']` returns ''. OK.\n\n2. `$(& $value 'name')` — script block invocation with `&`. `$value = { param($Key,$Default='') ... }`. `& $value 'name'` works. Inside a here-string expandable `@\"...\"@`, `$(...)` subexpressions work. `$(& $value 'name')` — hmm, is that valid inside a subexpression? `$(& $value 'name')` should parse. Let me simplify to avoid risk: replace the scriptblock approach with a plain function `Get-FormValueHtml`. Safer.\n\nLet me refactor: create function `Get-RehydrateValue { param($FormData, [string]$Key, [string]$Default='') }` that returns HtmlEncoded value or default.\n\nThen use `$(Get-RehydrateValue $v 'name')`.\n\nLet me rewrite that part of Invoke-ShowRegister.\n\n3. `Invoke-Profile` uses `$v = $Ctx.FormData` then `$v.ContainsKey(...)`. Fine.\n\n4. `$body = @{} + $Ctx.Body` — merging hashtables. Works.\n\n5. In `Invoke-ShowLogin` I reference `$global:Config.BarangayName` inside HtmlEncode within here-string — fine.\n\n6. `New-ErrorBody` has StatusCode param; `New-ErrorPage` calls it. In AuthController I call `New-ErrorBody -StatusCode 400` directly — fine.\n\n7. `Send-CsrfCookie` sets `bcr_csrf=1` — a fixed value. The login form's hidden input must contain `1`. But the form is rendered with `New-HtmlDocument` and no `-CsrfToken` passed, so `__CSRF__` → ''. That breaks the CSRF check (cookieToken='1' vs formToken='').\n\nI need the login/register forms to embed the cookie value. Simplest: set the hidden input value to `1` in the form template, and rely on the cookie being present. But the cookie is only set AFTER login/register (Send-CsrfCookie is called on success). Need it set before rendering the form too.\n\nLet me restructure: set the `bcr_csrf` cookie on GET /login and GET /register (as a random token), and embed that same random value in the hidden input. So I need to pass the token to the view.\n\nCleanest approach: generate a CSRF token for anonymous visitors:\n- On GET /login or /register: read cookie `bcr_csrf`; if missing, create a random one, set cookie, and use it as the form token.\n- On POST: compare cookie vs form field.\n\nLet me add to Security.ps1:\n```powershell\nfunction Get-AnonymousCsrfToken { param($Ctx, $Response)\n    $existing = Get-RequestCookie -Request $Ctx.Request -Name 'bcr_csrf'\n    if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($existing)) { return $existing }\n    $token = ConvertTo-Base64Url (New-RandomBytes -Count 24)\n    try { $Response.Headers['Set-Cookie'] = \"bcr_csrf=$token; Path=/; Max-Age=43200; SameSite=Lax\" } catch {}\n    return $token\n}\n```\nAnd embed `$token` (HTML-encoded) into the login/register form hidden input.\n\nThen remove `Send-CsrfCookie` calls on success (or keep them harmlessly). Actually I'll remove them since not needed.\n\nWait — but the double-submit cookie must not be HttpOnly so... no, HttpOnly is fine for double-submit since server reads cookie. Actually for double-submit the attacker can't read the cookie cross-origin anyway due to same-origin policy, so HttpOnly is fine (actually HttpOnly makes it even safer). But we need to set the cookie before writing the body — that's fine, we set headers before Send-Html.\n\nSo in Invoke-ShowLogin: compute `$anonCsrf = Get-AnonymousCsrfToken -Ctx $Ctx` and put into the hidden input. But careful: Set-Cookie header must be added before response body write. Send-Html comes after. Good.\n\nLet me implement.\n\n8. `Invoke-ShowRegister` similar.\n\n9. `Protect-Page` has unused `$next` assignment; harmless but let me clean.\n\n10. `Get-InitialsBadges` returns avatar span. OK.\n\n11. In Views New-NavBarHtml the `$actions` for anonymous uses a single-quoted here-string `@'...'@` — good, no interpolation needed.\n\n12. `$global:Config.Tagline`, `ShortDescription` are unused so far — will use in landing page. Note the `@'...'@` with `''` escapes for apostrophes: I wrote `Barangay San Isidro''s` inside a single-quoted here-string — in a single-quoted here-string, `''` stays as literal two apostrophes! That's a bug. In here-strings, no escaping is processed. So `Isidro''s` would render as `Isidro''s`. Let me fix by writing plain apostrophes.\n\nLet me fix Config.ShortDescription.\n\nLet me now make these fixes.\n\nAlso, `$global:Config.ShortDescription` uses `@'...'@` — multi-line with newlines. For the landing page I'd rather have a single-line description. Let me restructure into separate single-line fields.\n\nLet me fix Core.ps1 config.",
