      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Sign-in works now — `POST /login → 302 → /dashboard → 200`, admin nav and dashboard rendered, zero console errors.\n\n## What was wrong\n\nWhen you signed in, the server **crashed instead of loading the page**, so the browser got an empty response — \"nothing happens\".\n\nTwo bugs stacked up:\n\n**1. The real crash.** Your account name was a single word with no space (`qwdsadasfdsfsdfds`). In `Get-Initials`, this line:\n\n```powershell\n$parts = ($Name -split '\\s+') | Where-Object { $_ -ne '' }\n```\n\nWith two or more words, `$parts` is an array and `$parts[0]` is a String. With **one** word, PowerShell collapses it to a plain String — so `$parts[0]` returns a `Char`, and `Char` has no `.Substring()`. Result: `500` on every page.\n\nThe server log confirmed it:\n```\n! 500 /dashboard :: Method invocation failed because [System.Char] does not contain a method named 'Substring'.\n```\n\n**2. Why the error page was blank too.** The 500 handler renders the navigation bar, which calls `Get-Initials` again — so the error page crashed the same way, and nothing was ever sent to your browser. That's why it looked like nothing happened.\n\nMy test suite missed this because every test account had a multi-word name.\n\n## Fixes applied\n\n- `Get-Initials` now wraps the split in `@()` so it always gets an array, and handles 1, 2 and n-word names\n- The 500 handler now falls back to a minimal standalone HTML page (then plain text) if rendering ever fails again — an error page can never be blank\n- Same class of bug fixed in the \"Welcome back, {name}\" greeting on the dashboard and landing page\n\n## Why my first fix didn't work\n\nI patched the code and restarted — but the tests still failed identically. A **stale server process from before the fix was still holding port 8080**, so each new server failed to bind and exited silently while the old code kept serving. Killing the real process (PID 1460) made the fix take effect immediately.\n\n## Verification\n\nI added a regression section to `test-app.ps1` covering single-word, one-character, apostrophe, hyphenated and accented names (`Zoë`, `Muñoz`, `François`):\n\n```\nALL TESTS PASSED   (243 checks)     was 184\nRENDER CHECK: all pages clean\n500s: NONE\n```\n\nOne more trap I hit and fixed: PowerShell 5.1 reads `.ps1` files as ANSI unless they have a UTF-8 BOM, so a literal `ë` in the test file got mangled. All `.ps1` files are now pure ASCII, and the test builds accented names from code points instead.\n\nThe server is running at **http://localhost:8080/**. If you had an account with a single-word name, sign in again — it works now. If it still misbehaves, tell me the exact name you used and I'll check it directly."
