        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Done. The web version is built, tested, and ready to deploy.\n\n## What I built\n\n`github-pages/` — a **static** site (HTML + CSS + vanilla JavaScript, no framework, no build step) that deploys to GitHub Pages with just a file upload.\n\nThe PowerShell version is untouched and still there if you want it locally.\n\n### How it maps to your requirements\n\n| Requirement | Implementation |\n|---|---|\n| **Landing page** | Business name, description, 8-category services menu, **12 images**, CTAs (*Create a free account* / *Sign in* / *Report a concern*), how-it-works, contacts, FAQ. Responsive. |\n| **Register / Login / Logout** | Name + email + password. Session token with 8-hour expiry. |\n| **Passwords hashed** | PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256, **150,000 iterations**, random 16-byte salt per account. Verified: `Admin@12345` never appears in storage. |\n| **Two access levels** | Resident sees **only their own** reports; Admin sees everything and manages all accounts. Enforced in the data layer, not just hidden in the UI. |\n| **Protected pages** | Six routes redirect anonymous visitors to login and return them afterwards. Admin routes give 403 to residents. |\n| **CRUD** | Full Create/Read/Update/Delete on **Complaints** *and* **user accounts**. |\n| **Validation** | Friendly messages beside each field, all problems reported at once, input preserved. |\n| **Persistence** | `localStorage`. Survives restarts, with JSON export/import so you can move data. |\n| **Delete confirmation** | Confirmation page **plus** a modal, and cancelling genuinely cancels. |\n\n## Bugs I found and fixed while testing\n\n1. **Login never worked** — `verifyPassword` read `record.hash` but user objects store `passwordHash`, so every login returned false.\n2. **Registering didn't sign you in** — you were bounced straight back to the login page.\n3. **403/404 pages rendered `[object Object]`** — the router wrapped already-complete view objects.\n4. **One Promote click demoted the user** — a click listener re-attached on every navigation, so it fired repeatedly. Also fixed the ordering so \"No\" on the confirmation actually cancels.\n5. **Service icons never loaded** — `loading=\"lazy\"` never fetched them.\n6. **Demo table overflowed** on every phone width.\n7. **Data page crashed** — called a function that didn't exist.\n8. **Login messages leaked which emails had accounts** — now identical for unknown-email and wrong-password.\n\n## Verification\n\n```\nUNIT 69 checks, 0 failures\nUI 100 checks, 0 failures\nTOTAL 169 checks, 0 failures\nLighthouse: accessibility 1.0 · best practices 1.0 · SEO 1.0\nResponsive: 32 page/width combos, no overflow, no broken images\nConsole: 0 errors on every route\n```\n\nI also verified it deployed at `/barangay-complaint-system/` — a subdirectory, exactly like a GitHub Pages project site. All paths are relative, so it works at both `user.github.io/` and `user.github.io/repo/`.\n\n## To deploy\n\n1. Create a public GitHub repo\n2. **Add file → Upload files** → drag in the contents of `github-pages`\n3. **Settings → Pages** → Source: *Deploy from a branch* → branch `main`, folder `/ (root)`\n\nLive at `https://YOUR-USERNAME.github.io/YOUR-REPO/` in about 30 seconds.\n\nTwo things to be upfront about: data lives **in each browser**, so two people on different machines won't see each other's reports (that's why I built the export/import page), and since there's no server, the admin/resident split is enforced faithfully in the browser but isn't tamper-proof. Both are explained in the README."
